---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import ZilLab from "../../components/ZilLab.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import SourceNote from "../../components/SourceNote.astro";
import SourcePanel from "../../components/SourcePanel.astro";
import RoomNav from "../../components/RoomNav.astro";
import { gh } from "../../lib/openzfs";
import { room, roomUrl, roomNumber } from "../../lib/rooms";

// The question-first format (DECISIONS.md D21.1, D27.1): the question, a plain
// answer, the figure, then the code in Source notes. Links to the code live in
// the notes only.
const lang = "en";
const r = room("zil");
const Z = "module/zfs/zil.c";
const ZL = "module/zfs/zfs_log.c";
const L = {
  itxAssign: gh(ZL, 693),
  tput: gh(Z, 2216, 2217),
  immediate: gh(Z, 2191),
  hasSlogs: gh("module/zfs/spa_misc.c", 2741, 2744),
  downgrade: gh(ZL, 645, 647),
  createWait: gh(Z, 1034, 1056),
  waiters: gh(Z, 1508, 1524),
  open: gh(Z, 1758),
  close: gh(Z, 1880),
  nextLwb: gh(Z, 1931),
  ready: gh(Z, 1972),
  issued: gh(Z, 2113),
  writeDone: gh(Z, 1605),
  flushDone: gh(Z, 1493),
  nextBlk: gh(Z, 2039),
  slogFlag: gh(Z, 2118, 2119),
  allocRest: gh("module/zfs/zio.c", 4567, 4595),
  vdevLogMg: gh("module/zfs/vdev.c", 1571, 1578),
  clean: gh(Z, 2862),
  parse: gh(Z, 499, 572),
  claimWrite: gh(Z, 634, 655),
  spaClaim: gh("module/zfs/spa.c", 5697, 5699),
  flags: gh("include/sys/zil.h", 76, 77),
  mount: gh("module/os/linux/zfs/zfs_vfsops.c", 795, 801),
  noReplay: gh(Z, 4801, 4803),
  destroy: gh(Z, 4822, 4823),
  keepFirst: gh(Z, 4181, 4192),
  reuse: gh(Z, 1004, 1011),
};
---

<Base
  lang={lang}
  roomId="zil"
  title="What does fsync() wait for? The ZIL and the SLOG · Uberblok"
  description="fsync() on ZFS waits for a few records written to an intent log, not for the txg: the log is a chain of blocks on a SLOG or on the pool, freed once the txg syncs and replayed after a power cut. Interactive, with the OpenZFS code."
>
  <article class="wrap">
    <header class="room-head">
      <p class="kicker">Room {roomNumber(r.id)} · {r.title.en}</p>
      <h1>What does fsync() wait for?</h1>
      <div class="prose answer">
        <p class="lead">
          A program that calls fsync on a file asks for one promise: when the call returns, the data it wrote survives a
          power cut. A database asks for it on every commit. ZFS writes changes to the pool in batches called transaction
          groups, or txgs, and commits a txg only every few seconds, after rewriting every block on the path from the
          change up to the root of the pool. Waiting for that on every fsync would be slow, and would write far more than
          the program changed.
        </p>
        <p>
          So each dataset also keeps an intent log, the ZIL. Every write leaves a small record of itself in memory: which
          file, where, how long, and often the data. When the program calls fsync, ZFS writes that file's records at once
          to a log block on disk, waits for the disk to flush its cache, and returns. The txg still writes the change to
          the pool later, as usual, and once it has, the log block is no longer needed and is freed. On a pool without a
          separate log device, a large write is not copied into the log: its data is written once, in place in the pool,
          and the record only points to it.
        </p>
        <p>
          The log is read only after a crash. When the pool is imported again, ZFS walks the chain of log blocks and keeps
          the pool from reusing them. When the dataset is mounted, it applies again every record whose txg never reached
          the pool, so every write that fsync returned for comes back. A write that was never synced or logged is lost,
          which is what its program accepted when it did not call fsync. A separate log device, called a SLOG, gives the
          log blocks a device of their own; what it gains depends on how fast that device is.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="lab">
      <h2 id="lab"><span class="n">1</span> Write, fsync, cut the power</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The figure is a new pool with one file whose blocks are 128 KiB. Txg 100 is open and the dataset has no log yet.
          Press <code>write()</code>, then <code>fsync()</code>, then Next step until <code>fsync()</code> returns. Each press
          shows one step: the line at the bottom of the figure says what it did, and a ring marks the part that changed.
          This first <code>fsync()</code> creates the log, which takes a txg sync, so it logs nothing: the write is already
          in the pool when the call returns.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then press <code>write()</code> and <code>fsync()</code> again. This time the record goes into a log block, and
          <code>fsync()</code> returns while txg 101 is still open. Cut the power, import the pool and mount the dataset,
          and the record is replayed. Start again and sync the txg before the power cut: the log has nothing left to
          replay. A write with no <code>fsync()</code> before the cut is lost.
        </p>
        <p>
          The panel at the top says what the next <code>write()</code> will put in its record. Try 128 KiB without a SLOG and
          then with one. Without a SLOG, the data is written in place and the record only points to it. With one, all the
          data goes into the log, split across two log blocks. With <code>logbias=throughput</code> the data is always
          written in place.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ZilLab lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="code">
      <h2 id="code">In the code</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        The figure follows nine places in the source. Open a Source note to see the lines, taken from release zfs-2.4.4.
      </p>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>A record for each write</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          <code>zfs_log_write</code> runs on every write. It fills a record, an itx, with what is needed to apply the write
          again: which file, where, how long, and how the data travels. Nothing is written to disk yet.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>zfs_log_write</code>, zfs_log.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={ZL}
            from={677}
            to={682}
            symbol="zfs_log_write"
            notes={[
              { from: 677, text: "How the data travels: copied into the record, copied into the log block later, or written in place, as the next note decides." },
              { from: 678, to: 680, text: "The file, the offset and the length: enough to apply the write again after a crash." },
              {
                from: 681,
                to: 682,
                text: `No block pointer yet: a record whose data is written in place gets one when that block is written. The record is then <a href="${L.itxAssign}">queued under the write's txg</a>.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>In the record, or in place</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          <code>zil_write_state</code> decides how much of the data the record carries. A large block written once, in
          place, costs less than the same data written to the log and then to the pool, except when the pool has a SLOG.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>zil_write_state</code>, zil.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={Z}
            from={2227}
            to={2245}
            symbol="zil_write_state"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 2227,
                to: 2228,
                text: `In place only from <a href="${L.immediate}">32 KiB</a> and, for a write committed at once (<code>O_DSYNC</code>), only from half a block, since the next write might rewrite the same block. Before this test, <a href="${L.tput}"><code>logbias=throughput</code> or Direct I/O</a> always returns WR_INDIRECT.`,
              },
              {
                from: 2230,
                to: 2232,
                text: `A pool with a SLOG never writes in place. <a href="${L.hasSlogs}"><code>spa_has_slogs</code></a> asks whether the log class has a device, not whether it has room: a full SLOG still counts.`,
              },
              { from: 2233, to: 2238, text: "Special vdevs without a SLOG. The figure's pool has none." },
              {
                from: 2240,
                to: 2245,
                text: `WR_INDIRECT, written in place; WR_COPIED, copied into the record now, for a write committed at once; otherwise WR_NEED_COPY, copied into the log block when it is filled. A WR_COPIED write <a href="${L.downgrade}">over 7,680 bytes</a> becomes WR_NEED_COPY, since a copied record must fit in one log block.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>fsync() waits for one log block</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          <code>fsync()</code> calls <code>zil_commit</code>, and <code>zil_commit_impl</code> does the work: it writes the
          file's records and sleeps until the block that holds the last of them is on stable storage.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>zil_commit_impl</code>, zil.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={Z}
            from={4097}
            to={4101}
            symbol="zil_commit_impl"
            notes={[
              { from: 4097, to: 4098, text: "A commit waiter is queued behind every record of the file." },
              {
                from: 4100,
                text: `The records are packed into lwbs, log write blocks, and the lwbs are issued. A dataset with no log creates one first, and <a href="${L.createWait}">waits for a txg to sync</a> so that the header can name its first block: the first commit on a new log costs a txg.`,
              },
              { from: 4101, text: `The caller sleeps until the lwb that holds its waiter is written and <a href="${L.waiters}">the disk caches are flushed</a>.` },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>The seven states of an lwb</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          An lwb is a log block being built in memory. The figure marks its state on each lwb, in the order of
          <code>lwb_state_t</code>.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>lwb_state_t</code>, zil_impl.h</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path="include/sys/zil_impl.h"
            from={83}
            to={92}
            symbol="lwb_state_t"
            notes={[
              { from: 84, to: 85, text: `New, then <a href="${L.open}">opened</a> by its first record.` },
              {
                from: 86,
                text: `<a href="${L.close}">Closed</a> when full, or at the end of a commit, which also <a href="${L.nextLwb}">creates the next lwb</a>, still empty.`,
              },
              {
                from: 87,
                to: 88,
                text: `<a href="${L.ready}">Ready</a> once its records are filled in; <a href="${L.issued}">issued</a> once the next block is allocated.`,
              },
              {
                from: 89,
                to: 90,
                text: `<a href="${L.writeDone}">Write done</a>, then <a href="${L.flushDone}">flush done</a> once the disk caches are flushed: the commits waiting on it return.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Each block names the next</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          Before an lwb is written, <code>zil_lwb_write_issue</code> allocates the block of the next lwb and puts its
          address in this block's trailer. The log is a chain that starts at the header.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>zil_lwb_write_issue</code>, zil.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={Z}
            from={2086}
            to={2092}
            symbol="zil_lwb_write_issue"
            notes={[
              { from: 2087, text: `The next block, whose pointer <a href="${L.nextBlk}">goes into this block's trailer</a>, is born in an open txg.` },
              {
                from: 2088,
                to: 2091,
                text: "It takes this block's checksum seed, with the sequence word incremented. After a crash, the next block must match that checksum. The block that was allocated but never written does not, and ends the chain.",
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>The SLOG first</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          <code>zio_alloc_zil</code> allocates every log block, the first one of a chain included. It tries the SLOG first.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>zio_alloc_zil</code>, zio.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path="module/zfs/zio.c"
            from={4561}
            to={4565}
            symbol="zio_alloc_zil"
            notes={[
              { from: 4561, to: 4564, text: "The log class, the SLOG devices, first." },
              {
                from: 4565,
                text: `<code>slog</code> records whether that worked; it becomes <a href="${L.slogFlag}"><code>LWB_FLAG_SLOG</code></a> on the lwb. Otherwise the function <a href="${L.allocRest}">tries, in order</a>, the log space on special vdevs, the special vdevs, the log space every normal vdev <a href="${L.vdevLogMg}">sets aside for the ZIL</a>, then the normal vdevs. <code>logbias</code> does not change this order: with <code>throughput</code> the data is written in place, but the log block still comes from the SLOG.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>When the txg syncs, the log is freed</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          Once a txg has synced, the records of its writes are no longer needed. <code>zil_sync</code> runs during that
          sync and frees log blocks from the head of the chain.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>zil_sync</code>, zil.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={Z}
            from={4206}
            to={4214}
            symbol="zil_sync"
            notes={[
              { from: 4207, text: "The header's <code>zh_log</code>, where the chain starts, follows the head of the list." },
              {
                from: 4208,
                to: 4210,
                text: "An lwb stays if it is not flushed yet, or if its block or one of its records belongs to a later txg. The first one that stays stops the loop.",
              },
              {
                from: 4211,
                to: 4214,
                text: `Otherwise its block is freed. Records of the synced txg that never reached a log block are <a href="${L.clean}">dropped from memory</a>.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>After a power cut, the import claims the chain</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          At import, in the pool's first txg, <code>zil_claim</code> walks each dataset's chain. It only marks the blocks
          and counts the records; nothing is replayed yet.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>zil_claim</code>, zil.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={Z}
            from={1239}
            to={1246}
            symbol="zil_claim"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 1239,
                to: 1241,
                text: `<a href="${L.parse}"><code>zil_parse</code></a> walks the chain from <code>zh_log</code> and claims each block, so that the allocator does not hand it out again, and <a href="${L.claimWrite}">the blocks that WR_INDIRECT records point to</a>.`,
              },
              { from: 1242, text: `The claim txg: the pool's first txg, <a href="${L.spaClaim}">the last synced one plus one</a>.` },
              { from: 1243, to: 1244, text: "The highest block and record numbers claimed." },
              {
                from: 1245,
                to: 1246,
                text: `<a href="${L.flags}"><code>ZIL_REPLAY_NEEDED</code></a> if a record was found, or more than one block followed. The unwritten last block counts, so a log with nothing in it counts one.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>The mount replays what the txg did not keep</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          When the dataset is mounted, <code>zil_replay</code> walks the chain again and passes each record to
          <code>zil_replay_log_record</code>, which skips what is already in the pool.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>zil_replay_log_record</code>, zil.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={Z}
            from={4708}
            to={4712}
            symbol="zil_replay_log_record"
            notes={[
              { from: 4708, to: 4709, text: `A record already replayed by an earlier, interrupted replay is skipped. The mount <a href="${L.mount}">calls <code>zil_replay</code></a>.` },
              {
                from: 4711,
                to: 4712,
                text: `A record of a txg older than the claim txg is skipped: that txg synced, so its change is in the pool. Every other record is applied again, then <a href="${L.destroy}">the log is destroyed</a>. With nothing to replay, <code>zil_replay</code> <a href="${L.noReplay}">only destroys the log</a> and <a href="${L.keepFirst}">keeps its first block</a>, which the next commit <a href="${L.reuse}">writes into</a> without waiting for a txg.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>
      <SourcePanel lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section simplified" aria-labelledby="simplified">
      <h2 id="simplified">What the figure leaves out</h2>
      <p class="prose muted">
        The figure runs the functions above on one file of one dataset, with one writer and one txg in flight, synced at
        once in one pass. The power is cut between two actions, never in the middle of one. The file already spans several
        records, so its blocks are 128 KiB; a file of a single block smaller than <code>recordsize</code> has a smaller
        block, and the half-block rule uses it. The first block of a new chain is 4 KiB, as in the code; every later log
        block is 128 KiB, where the code sizes each block from the burst of records (<code>zil_lwb_plan</code>). So in a real
        chain records split at other places, and the byte counts in the figure are those of 128 KiB blocks. Each
        <code>write()</code> starts at the beginning of a file block. There is no I/O error, so the commit never falls back
        to waiting for a txg sync; the flush is one step for the whole lwb, without the per-vdev flush tree; the commit
        timeout never fires, because one writer closes its lwb at once. Writes of more than 64 MiB per commit
        (<code>zil_slog_bulk</code>) go to a SLOG at a lower priority; the Slim ZIL layout and encryption are not drawn.
        None of these changes how a write is logged, where a log block goes, or which writes are replayed. When a txg
        syncs, see <a href={roomUrl(lang, "txg")}>Transaction groups</a>; how the classes share the pool's space,
        <a href={roomUrl(lang, "metaslabs")}>Metaslabs and space maps</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
  <RoomNav lang={lang} roomId="zil" />
</Base>

